<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oc Eo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st–7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oc Eo is an archaeological site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, believed to be the main port of Funan. Excavations since 1944 have revealed a large town linked by canals, with Roman coins, Indian seals, Chinese mirrors and local jewellery. It shows how Funan connected the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> in the early centuries CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oc-eo.jpg" alt="Oc Eo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N%C3%BAi_Ba_Th%C3%AA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
